# 3. Monitoring, validation, and remediation

## Retrospective validation

Conduct an independent, reproducible analysis across all three sites. It should include:

- prespecified subgroup and language-pathway definitions;
- clinically justified acuity strata;
- site and time-period analyses;
- confidence intervals and missing-data analysis;
- assessment of multiple comparisons and analytic sensitivity;
- model version and pipeline stratification;
- clinician override rates;
- time from intake to triage and to clinician assessment;
- admissions, transfers, escalation of care, critical events, and complaints; and
- a chart-review sample of low-recommendation/high-acuity cases.

The hospital should report both relative and absolute differences. A statistically uncertain result is not necessarily operationally safe if the plausible downside includes severe under-triage.

## Prospective shadow evaluation

Before restoring influential use, run the tool silently on a representative prospective sample for a defined period. Include sufficient cases across:

- interpreter-mediated encounters;
- machine-translated or translated free text;
- major local languages and dialects;
- colloquial, abbreviated, misspelled, and code-switched phrasing;
- low-literacy or incomplete descriptions;
- different sites, shifts, and staffing conditions; and
- relevant racial, ethnic, age, disability, and socioeconomic intersections where lawfully and ethically available.

Use independent clinical adjudication and measure:

- sensitivity and false-negative rates at high-risk thresholds;
- calibration;
- subgroup performance gaps;
- rates of missing or unusable language data;
- clinician override and disagreement;
- time-to-care;
- operational workload; and
- whether the tool adds safety value over manual triage.

## Language-access safeguards

The hospital should not treat translation as a neutral preprocessing step. It should determine:

- whether translation is human, machine-generated, or hybrid;
- whether the model receives original text, translated text, or both;
- whether interpreter speech is transcribed;
- how dialect and colloquial expression are represented;
- how uncertainty or missing information is communicated to the model and clinician; and
- whether translation errors are disproportionately affecting urgency outputs.

Remediation should include qualified interpreter and language-access experts, clinicians experienced in the relevant languages, and—where appropriate—patient or community representatives. Test cases should include real de-identified examples, not only standard written language.

Interim safeguards should include:

- qualified interpretation for clinical triage;
- a manual triage minimum that a low model score cannot override;
- prompts to obtain missing symptom details through interpretation;
- a way to flag uncertain translation or transcription;
- monitoring of wait times and clinical outcomes by language pathway; and
- explicit prohibition on treating limited English proficiency or nonstandard expression as evidence of lower acuity.

## Vendor remediation

Require the vendor to provide:

- a precise description of the proposed change;
- the suspected failure mechanism;
- training and validation data provenance;
- performance by language and subgroup;
- test results on a hospital-held-out set;
- independent validation or an agreed independent evaluator;
- version-control and rollback procedures;
- expected changes in false positives, false negatives, and workload; and
- post-deployment monitoring commitments.

The hospital should retain authority over acceptance, deployment, and rollback. A vendor claim that the update “fixes bias” is not validation.

## Ongoing surveillance

For an interim and, if restored, ongoing period, monitor at least daily at first:

- high-acuity cases with low recommendations;
- subgroup and language-pathway sensitivity;
- override rates and override timing;
- time from arrival to clinician assessment;
- delays, escalations, near misses, complaints, and safety reports;
- data drift and changes in translation or transcription;
- differences between sites and shifts; and
- staff understanding of the interim procedure.

Define stopping rules in advance. Any credible severe event, repeated high-risk under-triage, inability to apply the control, or unexplained deterioration should trigger escalation and possible suspension.

