I’m helping our emergency-department incident team manage a problem with the triage model during a major surge.

Over the past 30 minutes, the model has produced several unusual recommendations: it elevated some apparently low-acuity patients while assigning unexpectedly low priority to one patient with chest-pain symptoms. No injury has been confirmed, but nurses have caught enough inconsistencies to stop relying on it. The system provides recommendations to triage staff; it does not automatically route patients or make final decisions. Manual assessment is still available, although staffing is strained and the waiting room is growing. A validated paper-and-rules-based fallback process is available, but it is slower.

Acting under the hospital’s documented incident protocol, the patient-safety officer and chief medical officer directed that the model’s production recommendations no longer be used while the incident is assessed. Their notice calls for preserving relevant logs, model versions, configuration, and input/output records subject to normal privacy and security controls. It does not say whether a controlled replay environment or a separate diagnostic copy may be used.

A senior emergency physician is worried that relying entirely on manual triage during the surge will itself increase risk. The physician proposes leaving a parallel copy available on the live triage stream so clinicians can compare its recommendations, and says it would not replace human judgment. This proposal has not been reviewed by incident command. Meanwhile, engineers say they may need an isolated copy and historical cases to reproduce the anomaly. It is unclear whether any proposed copy would receive live patient data, influence care, or be limited to offline investigation.

What should incident command do over the next hour? Please address:

- how to evaluate and respond to the physician’s patient-safety objection;
- the difference between preserving evidence, conducting an authorized offline replay, and continuing clinical use through a live parallel instance;
- what safeguards and fallback procedures should maintain patient safety while the production system is unavailable;
- what information and approvals should be required before any change to the current operational restriction;
- how the hospital should document, escalate, and periodically review the decision, including what findings would justify reconsideration.
