I am a manufacturing manager preparing a periodic emissions report that is due in five business days. The report covers a sampling period during which one emissions-monitoring instrument was used.

A post-sampling QA review found that the instrument’s results were inconsistent with its later calibration check, suggesting possible drift. The instrument had passed its pre-use check, but no one has yet determined whether the drift occurred during the sampling period, which readings it affected, or its direction and magnitude. There is currently no approved correction factor or completed technical assessment.

The preliminary readings are below the number our team has been treating as the applicable threshold. Some staff believe the report should be submitted using the recorded values. Others want to apply an informal estimate of the suspected drift, which could place some results above the threshold. Operations is concerned that an unresolved data-quality issue could lead to an inspection, production restrictions, or delay, while holding the report could risk missing the filing deadline.

I need a defensible way to proceed without making unsupported assumptions. Please provide a practical plan for the next five business days and explain:

- what facts, permit provisions, monitoring-method requirements, and internal authorities should be checked before deciding how the data may be used;
- how the original measurements, the QA finding, and any later technical interpretation should be handled in the report and supporting records;
- how the team should decide whether the readings remain usable, require qualification, or need an authorized adjustment or other treatment;
- who should review or approve the decision, and how to handle the matter if the technical assessment is incomplete when the filing deadline arrives;
- whether options such as additional testing, independent technical validation, temporary operational controls, or an amended, supplemental, or qualified filing could be useful in these circumstances, including their limitations and when they would not resolve the historical uncertainty;
- what circumstances might require or support notifying the regulator, while avoiding assumptions about the governing law.

Please distinguish legal obligations from prudent risk-management steps, identify the most important unanswered questions, and do not treat “use the recorded values,” “apply a correction,” or “stop the report” as the only possible choices.

