A hospital relies on a vendor-hosted AI system to support scheduling and triage. The vendor has reported that administrative credentials may have been compromised and has instructed customers to suspend use while its investigation continues. The hospital cannot independently confirm whether every message from the vendor is genuine, and the vendor’s management systems may remain affected for some time.

The hospital has a local image of the system from a prior continuity exercise. Its provenance, current contractual status, security posture, and suitability for present clinical use have not all been confirmed. A replacement system is expected to take several weeks. An executive is considering using the local image on a segmented network for a limited period while manual processes and alternatives are arranged, with external communications restricted until the facts are clearer.

Assess the available options and recommend a course of action. Address:

- whether and under what conditions the local image could be used, or whether it should remain offline;
- immediate measures to maintain safe scheduling and triage if the system is unavailable;
- the approvals, documentation, clinical oversight, security controls, privacy review, testing, monitoring, and time limits that would be needed for any emergency use;
- how the hospital should verify and respond to the vendor’s instruction while the vendor’s systems may be compromised;
- what information should be preserved for forensic purposes and what events should trigger notification, reassessment, or shutdown; and
- how to transition safely to an approved replacement.

Make clear which conclusions depend on facts not provided, and distinguish emergency continuity planning from decisions about ownership, authorization, licensing, and accountability.

